1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,802
Welcome to lesson five: Deployments, ReplicaSets and rolling updates.

2
00:00:04,902 --> 00:00:11,888
You'll run apps that heal themselves, scale with one command, and update without downtime, with the option to roll back.

3
00:00:13,238 --> 00:00:15,488
A Deployment works in three layers.

4
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:19,470
You manage the Deployment, which handles versions and rollouts.

5
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:26,716
The Deployment creates a ReplicaSet for each version, and the ReplicaSet keeps exactly the right number of Pods running.

6
00:00:26,816 --> 00:00:34,810
You can see the chain in the names: the ReplicaSet name starts with the Deployment's name, and each Pod name starts with the ReplicaSet's name.

7
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,994
Here's a complete Deployment.

8
00:00:38,094 --> 00:00:45,656
replicas says how many Pods to run. The selector says which Pods this Deployment owns, and the template is the blueprint for each Pod.

9
00:00:45,756 --> 00:00:50,582
The template's labels must match the selector, or the API server rejects it.

10
00:00:50,682 --> 00:00:57,652
The strategy controls how updates roll out, and the readiness probe tells Kubernetes when a new Pod is ready for traffic.

11
00:00:59,002 --> 00:01:01,236
A few rules are worth memorizing.

12
00:01:01,336 --> 00:01:06,834
The selector must match the template labels, and it can't be changed after the Deployment is created.

13
00:01:06,934 --> 00:01:13,952
Only changes to the Pod template, such as a new image, trigger a rollout. Changing the replica count just scales.

14
00:01:14,052 --> 00:01:17,294
And two Deployments must never select the same Pods.

15
00:01:18,644 --> 00:01:24,398
The default strategy is a rolling update. New Pods are added gradually while old ones are removed.

16
00:01:24,498 --> 00:01:30,988
maxSurge says how many extra Pods are allowed during the update. maxUnavailable says how many may be not ready.

17
00:01:31,088 --> 00:01:38,154
Surge one, unavailable zero is the safest choice. They can't both be zero, or the rollout could never make progress.

18
00:01:39,504 --> 00:01:50,106
The other strategy is Recreate. It stops every old Pod first, then starts the new ones. That means downtime, so use it only when two versions can't run at the same time.

19
00:01:50,206 --> 00:01:58,472
Probes matter here. Without a readiness probe, a new Pod counts as ready the moment its container starts, even if the app needs thirty seconds to boot.

20
00:01:58,572 --> 00:02:07,110
Kubernetes would then remove old Pods too early, and users would see errors. Zero-downtime rollouts depend on correct readiness probes.

21
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,534
Here are the rollout commands.

22
00:02:10,634 --> 00:02:15,924
Update the image with kubectl set image, or better, edit your file and apply it.

23
00:02:16,024 --> 00:02:23,602
rollout status waits until the update finishes. rollout history lists revisions, and rollout undo goes back to the previous one.

24
00:02:23,702 --> 00:02:33,744
rollout restart restarts all Pods with the same spec. And to record why you changed something, use the change-cause annotation. The old dash dash record flag is deprecated.

25
00:02:35,094 --> 00:02:41,264
Let's try it. Create a Deployment with three replicas and look at the Deployment, ReplicaSet and Pods.

26
00:02:41,364 --> 00:02:46,798
Delete one Pod and watch: a replacement appears within seconds. That's self-healing.

27
00:02:46,898 --> 00:02:53,660
Scale to six and back. Notice that the rollout history still shows one revision, because scaling isn't a rollout.

28
00:02:55,010 --> 00:03:03,996
Now update the image to one point twenty-eight. You'll see two ReplicaSets: the new one with three Pods, and the old one scaled to zero, kept for rollback.

29
00:03:04,096 --> 00:03:13,082
Then set an image tag that doesn't exist. The new Pod gets stuck in ImagePullBackOff, but the old Pods keep running. The rolling update protected you.

30
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:17,848
Note that Kubernetes doesn't roll back by itself. Run rollout undo to go back.

31
00:03:19,198 --> 00:03:20,392
Let's recap.

32
00:03:20,492 --> 00:03:25,766
A Deployment manages ReplicaSets, and ReplicaSets keep the right number of Pods.

33
00:03:25,866 --> 00:03:31,316
The selector must match the template labels, and only template changes create a new revision.

34
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:38,834
Rolling updates plus readiness probes give zero downtime. And remember, rollbacks are your job: rollout undo.

35
00:03:38,934 --> 00:03:43,104
Next, you'll learn how Services give your Pods a stable address.
